<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nationalist faction (Spanish Civil War)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nationalists were the side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> that rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> in July 1936, uniting army officers, the Falange, monarchists, Carlists and conservative Catholics. Led from October 1936 by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> and aided by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, they won the war in April 1939. Their victory established Franco's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, which lasted until his death in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nationalist-faction-spanish-civil-war.jpg" alt="Nationalist faction (Spanish Civil War)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Durero · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Bando_Nacional_(1936%E2%80%931938).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Nationals (nacionales) was coined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> after a clandestine Spanish delegation led by Francisco Arranz asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for materiel on 24 July 1936, to give German help to the rebels a cloak of legitimacy. The rebels, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, Enrique Pla y Deniel, had already called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, took to it at once; their opponents called them fascistas or facciosos. Their support came mainly from the conservative upper class, liberal professionals, religious organisations and landowning farmers, above all in rural areas such as Old Castile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Falange Española, founded by José Antonio Primo de Rivera, merged in 1934 with the Juntas de Ofensiva Nacional-Sindicalista of Ramiro Ledesma Ramos, and by September 1936 its 35,000 volunteers made up 55 percent of the faction's civilian forces. The CEDA of José María Gil-Robles gave its electoral funds to General Emilio Mola, the Carlists under Manuel Fal Condé raised the Requetés, at their peak 42,000 strong, and the assassination of the Alfonsist leader José Calvo Sotelo on 13 July 1936 helped legitimise the coup on the right.</p><p>In 1937 Franco's Unification Decree merged these movements into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons">Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS</a> under his own leadership, to the fury of many Falangists and Carlists. He then distanced the party from fascism, and it took up neotraditionalist National Catholicism. The historians Paul Preston and Julián Casanova nonetheless see the faction as a form of Spanish fascism built on the unity of the right and the military rebels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Army of Africa</a>, garrisoned in Spanish Morocco under Franco, combined the Spanish Foreign Legion with the Regulares, troops recruited in Spanish Morocco under Spanish officers. More than 13,000 Moroccan troops were airlifted to the mainland on 20 Junkers Ju 52 planes supplied by Hitler between July and October 1936, and an estimated one in five members of the army was killed in the war. About 47% of the Civil Guard joined the rebels.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> had 35,000 men of the Corpo Truppe Volontarie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by January 1937 under General Mario Roatta, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/condor-legion">Condor Legion</a>, tanks and aircraft. Portugal under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a> supplied ammunition and logistics and signed the Iberian Pact with Franco in 1939, while the Holy See was among the first states to recognise his government, by 1938.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nationalist faction (Spanish Civil War)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
